{% comment %}
  sections/overlays.liquid

  Everything that floats above the page and is not the cart or search: the toast,
  the quick option drawer, the image zoom modal and the compare drawer.

  They live in a section so a merchant can turn individual ones off, and so the
  Theme Editor can re-render them without touching the layout. Each is gated on
  the setting that makes it reachable, so nothing ships markup for a feature that
  is switched off.
{% endcomment %}

{% render 'toast-notification' %}

{%- comment -%}
  Quick add is a modal, not a drawer.

  It used to be `<quick-option-drawer class="quick-view drawer" data-placement="end">`
  and it is neither of those things. A drawer is somewhere a customer goes and
  comes back from — the cart, the menu, the filters — and sliding the page aside
  to ask which flavour they want turns one inline choice into a navigation. Quick
  add interrupts the page the customer is reading and hands it straight back, so
  it takes the centre of the screen and the modal's own entrance.

  The markup is `<modal-dialog>`'s, class for class: `.modal`, `.modal__dialog`,
  `.modal__panel`. Nothing here is a quick-add-shaped copy of a modal — the
  overlay behaviour, the backdrop, the entrance and the close button placement
  are the ones `assets/base.css` already gives every modal in the theme, and
  `.quick-add` adds only the width and the body's scroll.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if settings.enable_quick_add -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    The entrance and its length are the merchant's — **Theme settings › Product
    cards › Quick add modal**. They are attributes rather than a stylesheet
    variable because the movement is a WAAPI animation on the panel, built from
    keyframes in `assets/dialog.js`: a modal that is closed has no box to
    transition, so the entrance cannot be a CSS transition and the panel's
    resting transform has to be left alone between openings.

    `<modal-dialog>` clamps the duration and falls back to its own default for a
    name it does not know, so a stale saved value can never leave a modal that
    takes three seconds to appear or one that never arrives.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <quick-add-modal
    id="QuickAdd"
    class="quick-add modal"
    data-overlay-type="quick-add"
    data-animation="{{ settings.quick_add_animation | default: 'zoom-in' }}"
    data-animation-duration="{{ settings.quick_add_animation_duration | default: 320 }}"
  >
    <dialog class="modal__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'sections.quick_add.title' | t }}">
      <div class="modal__panel" data-ref="panel">
        <header class="modal__header">
          {%- comment -%}
            Visually hidden, and still here.

            The modal's visible heading is the product's own name, written by
            `sections/quick-add.liquid` into the body — which is what a customer
            needs to read, and which cannot be known until the fetch lands. This
            one stays because it is the heading a screen reader meets on entering
            the dialog, before the fetched content has been reached, and because
            removing it would leave the dialog's heading level empty.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <h2 class="modal__title visually-hidden">{{ 'sections.quick_add.title' | t }}</h2>
          {%- liquid
            assign quick_add_close_label = 'sections.quick_add.close' | t
          -%}
          {% render 'button',
             label: quick_add_close_label,
             id: 'QuickAddClose',
             class: 'close-quick-add',
             icon: 'close',
             variant: 'primary',
             size: 'small',
             icon_only: true,
             attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}
        </header>

        <div class="modal__body" data-ref="body" data-lenis-prevent>
          <p class="quick-add__loading">{{ 'sections.quick_add.loading' | t }}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  </quick-add-modal>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if settings.enable_image_zoom -%}
  <media-zoom-modal id="MediaZoom" class="media-zoom-modal modal">
    <dialog class="modal__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.open_zoom' | t }}">
      <div class="modal__panel" data-ref="panel">
        {%- liquid
          assign media_zoom_close_label = 'accessibility.close_zoom' | t
        -%}
        {% render 'button',
           label: media_zoom_close_label,
           id: 'MediaZoomClose',
           class: 'close-media-zoom',
           icon: 'close',
           variant: 'primary',
           size: 'small',
           icon_only: true,
           attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}

        <div class="media-zoom-modal__viewport" data-ref="viewport" data-lenis-prevent>
          {%- comment -%}
            `width` and `height` are required on every `<img>` - without them the
            browser has no aspect ratio to reserve and the layout shifts when the
            file arrives.

            This one has no meaningful values to give: the source is written by
            `media-zoom.js` when a customer opens the modal, and it is a different
            photograph every time. The placeholder square satisfies the ratio, and
            `auto` on both axes hands the used size straight back to the image, so
            it still renders at its natural dimensions - which is what the zoom
            pans and scales.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <img
            data-ref="image"
            alt=""
            width="1000"
            height="1000"
            style="inline-size: auto; block-size: auto;"
          >
        </div>

        {%- comment -%}
          Where the zoom level is announced.

          It used to be written as `aria-label` on `<media-zoom-modal>` itself.
          A custom element with no role is `generic`, and `aria-label` is
          prohibited there - the audit is right, and the attribute was doing
          nothing either way: a label on a container is read when focus enters
          it, not when its value changes, so the level was never announced.

          A live region is read on change, which is what a zoom control needs.
          `role="status"` is polite by definition, so it waits for a pause rather
          than interrupting.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="visually-hidden" role="status" data-ref="zoomStatus"></span>

        <div class="media-zoom-modal__controls">
          <button type="button" data-ref="zoomOut" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.zoom_out' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'minus' %}
          </button>
          <button type="button" data-ref="reset" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.reset_zoom' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'refresh' %}
          </button>
          <button type="button" data-ref="zoomIn" aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.zoom_in' | t }}">
            {% render 'icon', icon: 'plus' %}
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </dialog>
  </media-zoom-modal>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if settings.enable_compare -%}
  <product-compare
    id="ProductCompare"
    class="product-compare"
    data-section-id="compare"
    data-limit="{{ settings.compare_limit | default: 4 }}"
  >
    <button type="button" class="product-compare__bar" data-ref="open" hidden></button>
    <span class="visually-hidden" data-ref="count">0</span>

    <drawer-component
      id="CompareDrawer"
      class="compare-drawer drawer"
      data-placement="bottom"
    >
      <dialog class="drawer__dialog" data-ref="dialog" aria-label="{{ 'products.compare.drawer_title' | t }}">
        <div class="drawer__panel" data-ref="panel">
          <header class="drawer__header">
            <h2 class="drawer__title">{{ 'products.compare.drawer_title' | t }}</h2>
            {%- liquid
              assign compare_drawer_close_label = 'accessibility.close' | t
            -%}
            {% render 'button',
               label: compare_drawer_close_label,
               id: 'CompareDrawerClose',
               class: 'close-compare-drawer',
               icon: 'close',
               variant: 'primary',
               size: 'small',
               icon_only: true,
               attributes: 'data-overlay-close' %}
          </header>

          <div class="drawer__body" data-ref="body" data-lenis-prevent>
            <div data-ref="content" data-compare-content></div>
          </div>

          <footer class="drawer__footer">
            <button type="button" class="button button--secondary" data-ref="clear">
              {{ 'products.compare.clear_all' | t }}
            </button>
          </footer>
        </div>
      </dialog>
    </drawer-component>
  </product-compare>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:sections.overlays.name",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "t:sections.overlays.paragraph"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}